blend a row with neon

The cart insert and eject animations still dropped frames on 0054, most of
the ~95 missed latches a session. The 0053 trace named the draws: the cart
faces, scaled down from 304x343 to 237x268 and fading, at 2-4 ms each and
several a frame, against 0.9 ms when the same face sits still. Still, a
face is mostly copied a row at a time; fading, every pixel goes through
`blend`, one at a time, three rounded divisions by 255 each.

The blit's per-pixel loops now hand a whole row to `blend_row`, which on the
Flip is a NEON loop over eight pixels at a time: the channels pulled apart,
widened, `c * a + under * (255 - a)` per colour, the same rounded division
by 255 `div255` does, alpha added with saturation as `blend`'s min(255),
and a pixel opaque enough copied whole. A pixel of alpha 0 needs nothing:
the sums give the panel's own bytes back then, since div255(v * 255) is v.
The faded, scaled-down path -- the fading cart -- gathers its row with the
fade applied first, as the other paths already did, so it can be blended
as a row too. The profile's skip, copy and blend counts are taken from the
row instead.

Not yet measured on the Flip.

Tests, as armv7 under qemu: the row blend matches the per-pixel rule byte
for byte for every source alpha against a spread of colours and panel
alphas, at both thresholds, in rows that end mid-group; it writes nothing
past its row; and every existing blit reference test now runs through it.
Dropping the rounding on one channel fails seven tests, and copying only
above the threshold rather than at it fails four.

Applies on top of 0054, upstream 91499a9.

